#!/usr/bin/python3
# Generated by Ask CK PyTest Creator for AWPTCM-T33233 — Port - Auto Negotiation
# Standardized ART framework script. Frame is fixed; test logic is filled per step.
#
# ==== OBJECTIVE — expected results this script must demonstrate ====
#   - Link established between two ports both configured for auto-negotiation, on copper and
#   fibre media types, where supported. Speak with SVT for a list of supported tranceivers.
#   - Negotiated speed and duplex equal to the highest common capability advertised by both
#   link partners.
#   - Operational status of both ports reported as up, with matching speed and duplex on
#   each end of the link.
#   - Auto-negotiation as the default administrative setting on ports that support it,
#   present after a fresh start with no explicit configuration.
#   - Negotiated operational values distinguishable from configured values in the reported
#   port state, with negotiation mode shown as auto/enabled.
#   - Re-negotiation performed after link loss, cable reconnection, port disable/enable, or
#   device restart, returning the same negotiated result.
#   - Updated negotiated speed and duplex after a link partner's advertised capabilities
#   change, without requiring manual intervention.
#   - Auto-negotiation on a port whose partner is fixed-configured resolving to a link at a
#   compatible speed, with duplex behaviour consistent with the platform's fallback rules.
#   - No link, or a clearly reported fault state, when the two ends share no common
#   capability.
#   - Fibre ports negotiating in line with the capabilities of the inserted transceiver,
#   including after transceiver removal and insertion of a different type.
#   - Link state and negotiation transitions recorded in logs and available through
#   statistics, counters, and management interfaces.
#   - No unexpected link flapping, error counter growth, or resource leakage across repeated
#   negotiation cycles.
# ==================================================================

